# Inner products and the area of a rectangle {
  :label: paper
}

:abstract:

  We give a picture of the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality in the plane. Both
  sides of the inequality are areas of rectangles that share a side, and
  the smaller rectangle lies inside the larger one.

::

## The inequality
  {:label: sec-ineq}

Let $u$ and $v$ be vectors in $\mathbb{R}^2$. The projection of $u$ onto
the line through $v$ is never longer than $u$ itself, and multiplying both
lengths by $\|v\|$ gives the following result.

:theorem: {
  :label: thm-cs
  :title: Cauchy–Schwarz
}

  For all vectors $u$ and $v$ in $\mathbb{R}^2$, $|\langle u, v\rangle| \le \|u\|\,\|v\|$,
  with equality exactly when $u$ and $v$ lie on one line.

::

:html: {
  :label: fig-cs
  :path: figs/schwarz.html
}
:caption: Drag $u$ or $v$. Point at a side of the inequality to see it as a
rectangle of height $\|v\|$.
::

In :ref:fig-cs::, the shaded rectangles have the same height, and their
lengths along $v$ are the projection of $u$ and the length of $u$.
